{"context": "published", "host": "www.josephsmithpapers.org", "showHeader": true, "footerStyle": "mini", "statusCode": 200, "uri": "letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833", "page": "4", "is404": false, "gallery": [{"image": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_002_thumb.jpg", "page": "1", "label": "2", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833/1"}, {"image": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_003_thumb.jpg", "page": "2", "label": "3", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833/2"}, {"image": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_004_thumb.jpg", "page": "3", "label": "4", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833/3"}, {"image": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_005_thumb.jpg", "page": "4", "label": "5", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833/4"}], "tableOfContents": [], "summary": {"uri": "letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833", "series": "JSPPD", "urlTitleValue": "JSPPD3", "documentSeriesTitle": "Letter to Silas Smith, 26 September 1833", "editorialPageNumber": "5", "isPageNumberPresent": true, "searchUrl": "/published/jsp/searchable/papers/53991957/53991957-0004.xml", "clearText": "<html><head></head><body><div class=\"indent continuation wasptag\"> Saints in ancient days, were saved in the Kingdom of God; neither do I doubt but that they held converse and communion with Him<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"4796681028006346086\"><a href=\"#4796681028006346086\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans27\"><span id=\"documenttrans27\">27</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “Him,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “them.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> while they were in the flesh, as Paul said to his Corinthian brethren that the Lord Jesus showed Himself to above five hundred Saints at one time after His resurrection.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"3358247639660018858\"><a href=\"#3358247639660018858\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans28\"><span id=\"documenttrans28\">28</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">1 Corinthians 15:6.</p><hr></div></div></aside> Job said that he knew that his Redeemer lived and that he should see Him in the flesh in the latter days.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"9992898622294786397\"><a href=\"#9992898622294786397\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans29\"><span id=\"documenttrans29\">29</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Job 19:25.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Enoch walked with God and by faith was translated.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"15593794580051633572\"><a href=\"#15593794580051633572\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans30\"><span id=\"documenttrans30\">30</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 5:24.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Noah was a perfect man in his generation and also walked with God.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"7036540683755973423\"><a href=\"#7036540683755973423\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans31\"><span id=\"documenttrans31\">31</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 6:9. In the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history, this sentence was missing. Robert Campbell, a clerk in the Church’s Historian’s Office in the 1850s and 1860s, later inserted the following sentence into the history: “I may [believe] that Noah was a perfect man in his generation &amp; also walked with God.” In his role as clerk, Campbell may have become aware that Jesse Smith owned the original sent copy of this JS letter and added this sentence based on what appears in that version. (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Abraham communed with God and conversed with angels.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1154883646715395066\"><a href=\"#1154883646715395066\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans32\"><span id=\"documenttrans32\">32</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 17:1; 22:11, 15.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Isaac obtained a renewal of the covenant made to Abraham by the direct voice of the Lord.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16706111770240619571\"><a href=\"#16706111770240619571\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans33\"><span id=\"documenttrans33\">33</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 26:2–5.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Jacob conversed with holy angels, and heard the voice<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1344575742886962008\"><a href=\"#1344575742886962008\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans34\"><span id=\"documenttrans34\">34</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “voice,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “word.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> of his Maker, that he wrestled with the angel until he prevailed and obtained the blessing.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"10250969372878736727\"><a href=\"#10250969372878736727\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans35\"><span id=\"documenttrans35\">35</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 32:24–32.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Elijah was taken to Heaven in a chariot of fire with fiery horses.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16752856648326426578\"><a href=\"#16752856648326426578\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans36\"><span id=\"documenttrans36\">36</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See 2 Kings 2:11.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that the saints saw the Lord and conversed with Him face to face after His resurrection.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"213735586659624480\"><a href=\"#213735586659624480\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans37\"><span id=\"documenttrans37\">37</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Luke 24:13–51; John 20:19–31; and Acts 1:3–9.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that the Hebrew Church came to Mount Zion, and unto the city of the Living God the Heavenly Jerusalem, and to an innumerable company of angels. I may believe that they looked into eternity, and saw the Judge of all, and Jesus the Mediator of the New Covenant.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"9042048980469084488\"><a href=\"#9042048980469084488\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans38\"><span id=\"documenttrans38\">38</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Hebrews 12:22–24.</p><hr></div></div></aside> But will all this purchase an assurance for me, and<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"6394918841723028093\"><a href=\"#6394918841723028093\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans39\"><span id=\"documenttrans39\">39</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “and,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “or.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> waft me to the regions of eternal day and seat me down in the presence of the King of Kings<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"9268018241295048785\"><a href=\"#9268018241295048785\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans40\"><span id=\"documenttrans40\">40</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits the passage “and seat me down in the presence of the King of Kings.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> with my garments spotless pure and white?</div><div class=\"indent wasptag\">Or must I not rather obtain for myself by my own faith and diligence in keeping the commandments of the Lord, an assurance of salvation for myself? And have I not an equal privilege with the ancient Saints? And will not the Lord hear my prayers and listen to my cries as soon as he ever did to theirs, if I come to him in the manner they did? Or, is he a respecter of persons?<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"6311546964588568846\"><a href=\"#6311546964588568846\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans41\"><span id=\"documenttrans41\">41</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\"> See Acts 10:34.</p><hr></div></div></aside></div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">So I must close this subject for want of time, and I may with propriety say at the beginning</div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">We would be glad<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"3797540820358949447\"><a href=\"#3797540820358949447\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans42\"><span id=\"documenttrans42\">42</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “glad,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “pleased.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> to see you in <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"7535638871507039768\"><a href=\"#7535638871507039768\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"plc13\" place=\"plc13\" title=\"Kirtland Township, Ohio\">Kirtland</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Kirtland Township, Ohio\"><p>Located ten miles south of Lake Erie. Settled by 1811. Organized by 1818. Latter-day Saint missionaries visited township, early Nov. 1830; many residents joined Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. Population in 1830 about 55 Latter-day Saints and...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/place/kirtland-township-ohio\">More Info</a></div></div></aside>, we would be glad to see you embrace<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"12221997130488906661\"><a href=\"#12221997130488906661\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans43\"><span id=\"documenttrans43\">43</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “we would be glad to see you embrace,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history reads “and more pleased to have you embrace.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> the New Covenant and be one with us, we sometimes think you are now one with us in heart.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"12583871335407128202\"><a href=\"#12583871335407128202\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans44\"><span id=\"documenttrans44\">44</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits the passage “and be one with us, we sometimes think you are now one with us in heart.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside></div> <div class=\" center wasptag\">I remain yours affectionately</div> <div class=\"right wasptag\">Joseph Smith Jun</div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">To <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"11795520100259850202\"><a href=\"#11795520100259850202\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"per1011\" person=\"per1011\" title=\"Silas Smith\">Silas Smith</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Smith, Silas\"><p>1 Oct. 1779–13 Sept. 1839. Farmer. Born in Derryfield (now Manchester), Hillsborough Co., New Hampshire. Son of Asael Smith and Mary Duty. Moved to Topsfield, Essex Co., Massachusetts, by 1790. Moved to Tunbridge, Orange Co., Vermont, by 1800. Married first...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/person/silas-smith\">View Full Bio</a></div></div></aside><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13274534378776269216\"><a href=\"#13274534378776269216\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans45\"><span id=\"documenttrans45\">45</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits this address marker. (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> [p. 5]</div></body></html>", "expandedText": "<html><head></head><body><div class=\"indent continuation wasptag\"> Saints in ancient days, were saved in the Kingdom of God; <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>neither do I doubt but that they held converse and communion <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>with Him<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"2031900295496535281\"><a href=\"#2031900295496535281\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans27\"><span id=\"documenttrans27\">27</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “Him,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “them.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> while they were in the flesh, as Paul said to his <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Corinthian brethren that the Lord Jesus showed Himself to above <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>five hundred Saints at one time after His resurrection.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"9110521741772195511\"><a href=\"#9110521741772195511\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans28\"><span id=\"documenttrans28\">28</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">1 Corinthians 15:6.</p><hr></div></div></aside> <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Job said that he knew that his Redeemer lived and that he should <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>see Him in the flesh in the latter days.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"3215400574101214782\"><a href=\"#3215400574101214782\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans29\"><span id=\"documenttrans29\">29</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Job 19:25.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Enoch walked with God and by faith was translated.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"2254381010721273345\"><a href=\"#2254381010721273345\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans30\"><span id=\"documenttrans30\">30</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 5:24.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>believe that Noah was a perfect man in his generation and <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>also walked with God.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"10971933969489785128\"><a href=\"#10971933969489785128\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans31\"><span id=\"documenttrans31\">31</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 6:9. In the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history, this sentence was missing. Robert Campbell, a clerk in the Church’s Historian’s Office in the 1850s and 1860s, later inserted the following sentence into the history: “I may [believe] that Noah was a perfect man in his generation &amp; also walked with God.” In his role as clerk, Campbell may have become aware that Jesse Smith owned the original sent copy of this JS letter and added this sentence based on what appears in that version. (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Abraham com<span class=\"line-break hyphenate\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>muned with God and conversed with angels.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"8110739187144454443\"><a href=\"#8110739187144454443\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans32\"><span id=\"documenttrans32\">32</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 17:1; 22:11, 15.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Isaac obtained a renewal of the covenant made to Abraham <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>by the direct voice of the Lord.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"11285285803003817779\"><a href=\"#11285285803003817779\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans33\"><span id=\"documenttrans33\">33</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 26:2–5.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Jacob conver<span class=\"line-break hyphenate\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>sed with holy angels, and heard the voice<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1091887829858414619\"><a href=\"#1091887829858414619\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans34\"><span id=\"documenttrans34\">34</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “voice,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “word.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> of his Maker, that he <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>wrestled with the angel until he prevailed and obtained the bles<span class=\"line-break hyphenate\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>sing.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16536009676624396493\"><a href=\"#16536009676624396493\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans35\"><span id=\"documenttrans35\">35</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 32:24–32.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that Elijah was taken to Heaven in a chariot <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>of fire with fiery horses.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"7950557406945920596\"><a href=\"#7950557406945920596\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans36\"><span id=\"documenttrans36\">36</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See 2 Kings 2:11.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that the saints saw <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>the Lord and conversed with Him face to face after His resurrec<span class=\"line-break hyphenate\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>tion.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"9562665529970955583\"><a href=\"#9562665529970955583\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans37\"><span id=\"documenttrans37\">37</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Luke 24:13–51; John 20:19–31; and Acts 1:3–9.</p><hr></div></div></aside> I may believe that the Hebrew Church came to Mount Zion, <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>and unto the city of the Living God the Heavenly Jerusalem, <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>and to an innumerable company of angels. I may believe that <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>they looked into eternity, and saw the Judge of all, and Jesus <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>the Mediator of the New Covenant.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"15973058917550173164\"><a href=\"#15973058917550173164\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans38\"><span id=\"documenttrans38\">38</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Hebrews 12:22–24.</p><hr></div></div></aside> But will all this pur<span class=\"line-break hyphenate\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>chase an assurance for me, and<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"8137443888306909542\"><a href=\"#8137443888306909542\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans39\"><span id=\"documenttrans39\">39</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “and,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “or.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> waft me to the regions <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>of eternal day and seat me down in the presence of the <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>King of Kings<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"11369156930918593885\"><a href=\"#11369156930918593885\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans40\"><span id=\"documenttrans40\">40</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits the passage “and seat me down in the presence of the King of Kings.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> with my garments spotless pure and white?<span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span></div><div class=\"indent wasptag\">Or must I not rather obtain for myself by my own faith <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>and diligence in keeping the commandments of the Lord, an <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>assurance of salvation for myself? And have I not an <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>equal privilege with the ancient Saints? And will not the Lord <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>hear my prayers and listen to my cries as soon as he ever <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>did to theirs, if I come to him in the manner they did? <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Or, is he a respecter of persons?<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"17358766101457937288\"><a href=\"#17358766101457937288\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans41\"><span id=\"documenttrans41\">41</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\"> See Acts 10:34.</p><hr></div></div></aside></div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">So I must close this subject for want of time, and I <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>may with propriety say at the beginning</div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">We would be glad<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13863708782449857653\"><a href=\"#13863708782449857653\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans42\"><span id=\"documenttrans42\">42</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “glad,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “pleased.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> to see you in <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"17303124429238565822\"><a href=\"#17303124429238565822\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"plc13\" place=\"plc13\" title=\"Kirtland Township, Ohio\">Kirtland</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Kirtland Township, Ohio\"><p>Located ten miles south of Lake Erie. Settled by 1811. Organized by 1818. Latter-day Saint missionaries visited township, early Nov. 1830; many residents joined Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. Population in 1830 about 55 Latter-day Saints and...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/place/kirtland-township-ohio\">More Info</a></div></div></aside>, we would <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>be glad to see you embrace<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"12473313315987947334\"><a href=\"#12473313315987947334\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans43\"><span id=\"documenttrans43\">43</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “we would be glad to see you embrace,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history reads “and more pleased to have you embrace.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> the New Covenant and be one with us, <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>we sometimes think you are now one with us in heart.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16682092694981897203\"><a href=\"#16682092694981897203\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans44\"><span id=\"documenttrans44\">44</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits the passage “and be one with us, we sometimes think you are now one with us in heart.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside></div> <div class=\" center wasptag\">I remain yours affectionately</div> <div class=\"right wasptag\">Joseph Smith Jun</div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">To <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"6602416714823290375\"><a href=\"#6602416714823290375\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"per1011\" person=\"per1011\" title=\"Silas Smith\">Silas Smith</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Smith, Silas\"><p>1 Oct. 1779–13 Sept. 1839. Farmer. Born in Derryfield (now Manchester), Hillsborough Co., New Hampshire. Son of Asael Smith and Mary Duty. Moved to Topsfield, Essex Co., Massachusetts, by 1790. Moved to Tunbridge, Orange Co., Vermont, by 1800. Married first...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/person/silas-smith\">View Full Bio</a></div></div></aside><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"227573420721211374\"><a href=\"#227573420721211374\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans45\"><span id=\"documenttrans45\">45</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits this address marker. (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p><hr></div></div></aside> [p. 5]</div></body></html>", "calendarsOfDocuments": [], "versions": [{"versionTitle": "Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/236", "versionFeatured": ""}, {"versionTitle": "Letter to Silas Smith, 26 September 1833", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833/1", "versionFeatured": "true"}], "images": {"smallImageUrl": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_005_thumb.jpg", "mediumImageUrl": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_005_med.jpg", "largeImageUrl": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_005.jpg", "downloadImageUrl": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_005_download.jpg"}, "imageCredit": "", "facsimile": "", "dzUrlXml": "/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_005.xml", "classification": "Documents", "publishedStatus": "FinalContent", "accessionNumber": "7970", "requestedPageNumber": "4", "numberOfPages": "4", "editorialTitle": "Letter to Silas Smith, 26 September 1833", "cleanEditorialTitle": "Letter to Silas Smith, 26 September 1833", "date": "September 26, 1833", "handwriting": "Handwriting of Jesse Smith", "footnotes": [{"id": "2031900295496535281", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “Him,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “them.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 27, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "9110521741772195511", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">1 Corinthians 15:6.</p></body></html>", "number": 28, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "3215400574101214782", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">Job 19:25.</p></body></html>", "number": 29, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "2254381010721273345", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 5:24.</p></body></html>", "number": 30, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "10971933969489785128", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 6:9. In the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history, this sentence was missing. Robert Campbell, a clerk in the Church’s Historian’s Office in the 1850s and 1860s, later inserted the following sentence into the history: “I may [believe] that Noah was a perfect man in his generation &amp; also walked with God.” In his role as clerk, Campbell may have become aware that Jesse Smith owned the original sent copy of this JS letter and added this sentence based on what appears in that version. (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 31, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "8110739187144454443", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 17:1; 22:11, 15.</p></body></html>", "number": 32, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "11285285803003817779", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 26:2–5.</p></body></html>", "number": 33, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "1091887829858414619", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “voice,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “word.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 231</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 34, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "16536009676624396493", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Genesis 32:24–32.</p></body></html>", "number": 35, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "7950557406945920596", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See 2 Kings 2:11.</p></body></html>", "number": 36, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "9562665529970955583", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Luke 24:13–51; John 20:19–31; and Acts 1:3–9.</p></body></html>", "number": 37, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "15973058917550173164", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Hebrews 12:22–24.</p></body></html>", "number": 38, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "8137443888306909542", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “and,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “or.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 39, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "11369156930918593885", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits the passage “and seat me down in the presence of the King of Kings.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 40, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "17358766101457937288", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\"> See Acts 10:34.</p></body></html>", "number": 41, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "13863708782449857653", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “glad,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history has “pleased.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 42, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "12473313315987947334", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">Instead of “we would be glad to see you embrace,” the copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history reads “and more pleased to have you embrace.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 43, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "16682092694981897203", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits the passage “and be one with us, we sometimes think you are now one with us in heart.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 44, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "227573420721211374", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">The copy in <a href=\"/person/lucy-mack-smith\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Lucy Mack Smith</a>’s history omits this address marker. (<a href=\"/paper-summary/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845/1\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/lucy-mack-smith-history-1845\" poptitle=\"Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845\">Lucy Mack Smith, History, 1845, 232</a>.)</p></body></html>", "number": 45, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}], "meta": {"/paper-summary-PageTitle": "Letter to Silas Smith, 26 September 1833, Page 5", "/paper-summary-MetaTitle": "Letter to Silas Smith, 26 September 1833, Page 5", "/paper-summary-MetaDescription": "Saints in ancient days, were saved in the Kingdom of God; neither do I doubt but that they held conv...", "/paper-summary-MetaUrl": "/paper-summary/letter-to-silas-smith-26-september-1833/4", "/paper-summary-MetaImage": "/bc-jsp/content/jsp/images/7970/7970_ms1489_1_01_005_thumb.jpg"}, "scribes": [{"id": "per63", "name": "Frederick G. Williams"}], "printVolumeLocation": "<a href=\"http://www.josephsmithpapers.org/articles/documents-volume-3-february-1833-march-1834\" class=\"externalLink\" xmlns:srchdoc=\"https://ldsc.xmlref.com/local/ldschurch/searchDocuments\" xmlns:lds=\"https://ldsc.xmlref.com/local/ldschurch\"><span class=\"title\">JSP,</span> D3:301–308</a>"}, "legal": {}, "disableVerticalScroll": true, "documentURI": "/published/jsp/searchable/papers/53991957/53991957-0004.xml", "searchOptions": [{"name": "| This Document", "value": "document"}, {"name": "| Full Site", "value": "site"}]}